#
# 17 September 2026 — and the file has outgrown its name. Three more villages
# were searched the same way and three more households came out, none of them
# from Senj or Otočac. What this file actually is now is a REGISTER OF WHO LEFT
# THIS COAST, village by village, each with an exact birth date.
Klenovica|Frank Pavelić (Pavelich, Paelick)|2 Apr 1894|Klenovica, Coratia ??? / «Klenovica Jugo, Slavia»|wife Pauline b.25 Jan 1903; Frank 18 Aug 1921, Pauline 25 Jan 1923|The Bronx, NY — declared 1936, petitioned 1939
Grižane|Kuzma Knez|25 Sep 1893|Belgrad, Grizane, Croatia Austria|—|New York City 1923
Grižane|Mihovil Likarić|29 Sep 1885|Grizane, Jugoslavia|wife Katie b.25 Jan 1911 (see note); Veronika 29 May 1920, Anica 29 Jul 1921|New York City 1935
#
# TWO THINGS TO READ OFF THESE ROWS RATHER THAN PAST THEM.
#
# · KLENOVICA IS THE KALANJ VILLAGE, and the one man who filed from it is not a
#   Kalanj. He is also entered under TWO SURNAMES three years apart — Pavelich
#   on the 1939 petition, PAELICK on the 1936 declaration — with the same birth
#   date, the same wife and the same two children. That is the Žubrinić lesson
#   again: the spelling that finds a family is the one the American clerk wrote,
#   and an exact-match surname box will never find both halves of one man.
#
# · «BELGRAD, GRIZANE» IS NOT A MISTAKE. Belgrad is the other half of the
#   municipality's own name — Grižane-Belgrad — so the clerk wrote the hamlet
#   and then the parish, which is exactly the shape a house-number entry takes
#   in a register. It is a finding about how these clerks worked, not a slip.
#
# AND ONE IMPOSSIBILITY, LEFT AS WRITTEN. Likarić's wife Katie is indexed as
# born 25 January 1911 and his daughter Veronika on 29 May 1920, which would
# make her a mother at nine. One of the two is wrong and this archive does not
# know which, so both are copied as the index has them and neither is used.
